 ## Overview
-This family encompasses techniques that generate volumetric data or manage rendering performance dynamically, rather than relying solely on static, pre-computed 3D textures. It addresses the limitations of static data in terms of memory overhead, resolution limits, and interactivity. The methods differ from standard direct volume ray casting by either computing density analytically via procedural noise functions (e.g., Worley noise) during the ray march [2447-volumetric-smoke-rendering-with-worley-noise-and-light-maps.md][8240-procedural-volumetric-cloud-rendering-gpu-shader.md], applying spatial distortions to rays for focus+context [6767-gpu-accelerated-ray-casting-with-volumetric-lenses.md], modeling deformations via inverse displacement mapping [10659-displacement-mapping-for-volume-rendering.md], or dynamically selecting between multi-frame rate warping and parallel raycasting based on workload complexity [11338-dynamic-multi-frame-rate-rendering.md].
